1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,050
Listen to a talk in a biology class.

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:12,555
In the early 1900s, sea otters, marine mammals that feed on invertebrates including sea

3
00:00:12,555 --> 00:00:17,250
urchins, were nearly wiped out along the United States Pacific Coast by fur hunters.

4
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:23,000
With otters gone, urchin numbers exploded, destroying kelp forests.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,130
That kelp is a type of seaweed and it's sea urchins favorite food.

6
00:00:27,130 --> 00:00:33,160
This left countless marine species without the protective habitat that kelp forest provided to them.

7
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:37,120
In 1972, hunting sea otters was banned.

8
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:44,170
As otters made a comeback, urchin numbers dropped, kelp forest grew back, and biodiversity flourished.

9
00:00:44,170 --> 00:00:51,864
Scientists realized otters were a keystone species, organisms whose impact on ecosystems is far

10
00:00:51,864 --> 00:00:53,250
greater than their numbers suggest.

11
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:56,804
A similar case occurred in Yellowstone National Park.

12
00:00:56,804 --> 00:01:01,020
Wolves, the park's top predator, had been eradicated.

13
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:06,149
Populations of elk, a deer-like animal and a favorite prey for wolves, soared

14
00:01:06,149 --> 00:01:10,095
and their huge populations damaged local vegetation, including the Willow

15
00:01:10,095 --> 00:01:14,040
and Aspen trees for which the park had been famous.

16
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:19,220
In the 1990s, wolves were reintroduced to the park to get the elk population under control.

17
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:24,080
But the wolves didn't just cut elk numbers, they changed elk behavior.

18
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:30,000
Afraid of being eaten by wolves, the elk avoided stream side areas where they're especially vulnerable.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,259
This helped Willow and Aspen trees reestablish themselves there,

20
00:01:33,259 --> 00:01:37,243
and the trees brought back Beavers, animals that live in streams

21
00:01:37,243 --> 00:01:40,140
and build dams to create ponds for themselves.

22
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:47,763
The Beaver dams created habitats for fish, birds and amphibians, a huge cascading effect

23
00:01:47,763 --> 00:01:51,030
because of this other keystone species.
